          <fr:mainmatter>
    
    
    <fr:tree show-metadata="true" numbered="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:uri>https://mkiyoaki.github.io/kiyoaki/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>kiyoaki</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/kiyoaki/</fr:route><fr:title text="Yifei Shi">Yifei Shi</fr:title><fr:taxon>Person</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="institution"><fr:link href="https://www.cam.ac.uk/" type="external">University of Cambridge</fr:link></fr:meta><fr:meta name="orcid">0009-0008-1488-187X</fr:meta><fr:meta name="position">Research Assistant</fr:meta><fr:meta name="external">https://mkiyoaki.github.io</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><html:p>
    I am an MPhil student in <html:em>Advanced Computer Science</html:em> and a Research Assistant at the <fr:link href="https://www.cam.ac.uk/" type="external">University of Cambridge</fr:link>, where I work on reasoning verification and verifier-guided post-training for large language models. 
    My current research studies how verification objectives and model architectures affect both verification performance and the representations learned by language models.
</html:p><html:p>
    Prior to Cambridge, I was a Research Assistant at <fr:link href="https://www.shlab.org.cn/" type="external">Shanghai Artificial Intelligence Laboratory</fr:link>, where I worked on reinforcement-learning methods that connect LLM reasoning with formal verification. I completed my undergraduate degree in <html:em>Computer Science</html:em> at <fr:link href="https://www.kcl.ac.uk/" type="external">King's College London</fr:link> with First Class Honours and an alumni of the Google DeepMind Education Programme cohort.
</html:p><html:p>
    My research interests lie in <html:strong>Natural Language Processing</html:strong> and the <html:strong>post-training of Large Language Models</html:strong>, particularly in how model behaviours can be specified, verified, and improved. 
    My current work focuses on reasoning verification, while I am increasingly interested in extending verification beyond settings with explicit notions of correctness to open-ended interactions, where properties such as behavioural fidelity must instead be grounded in natural-language specifications or protocols.
</html:p><html:p>
    More broadly, I aim to study how verification signals can be used both to evaluate and improve language models through post-training and reinforcement learning.
    I am also interested in mechanistic analyses of verifiers, particularly whether their judgments reflect the intended notion of validity or fidelity rather than exploitable shortcuts.
</html:p><html:p>
    This website is built by <fr:link href="https://www.forester-notes.org/index/index.xml" type="external">Forester</fr:link>.
    You may navigate this site by pressing <html:code>Ctrl + K</html:code>. 
</html:p></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  
    
    
